## In-memory complete profile Export artifact evidence

The new `atrament_profile_export_assembly` application combines validated
canonical manifest JSON, an exact caller-supplied payload set and stored-only
ZIP framing into one owned artifact. It sorts paths without normalization,
requires a one-to-one declared/supplied inventory and verifies every member's
byte length before comparing its SHA-256 digest. Only a fully admitted set can
produce a complete archive, its root fingerprint and its archive fingerprint.

An integration fixture decodes the resulting ZIP root and verifies every
member through the independent source-attached Import path. Negative fixtures
exercise missing and unexpected members, duplicate or divergent names,
early/late digest mismatch, length-before-digest precedence and resource caps.
A generated 256-case corpus proves canonical byte identity under manifest and
payload reordering and rejects corrupted members without partial artifacts.

This is a pure **in-memory** profile artifact, not an Export command. A trusted
caller-selected path adapter, durable atomic commit, overwrite/retry handling,
section encoders and session lifecycle binding still need implementation.

The complete profile artifact assembly now also provides exact ZIP byte-count
planning and caller-owned ZIP emission using the same typed-manifest, inventory,
length, digest and ZIP32 gates as owned assembly. A short buffer is refused
before any destination mutation; surplus capacity is untouched and the returned
root/full-archive SHA-256 fingerprints cover only emitted source bytes.

Two additional fixtures prove atomic refusal of wrong bytes, resource limits
and undersized caller buffers. The 256-case canonical corpus now compares all
three paths byte-for-byte and confirms that even later corrupted payloads
cannot mutate an already owned destination. The writer still does not establish
selected destination authority or an atomic filesystem commit.

The assembly gate now runs the stored-ZIP count, declared byte-budget and ZIP32
preflight **before** SHA-256 work on potentially large supplied payload bodies.
Exact path inventory is still matched first, and admitted archives retain their
length-before-digest failure priority. A focused three-writer regression
confirms
that an unaffordable archive fails by typed ZIP budget rather than hashing
corrupt member bytes; a sufficient budget still detects the digest mismatch.
No output or caller-owned buffer is affected by these refusals.

A Linux full-member Export session integration now commits a stored profile
with one syntax-valid JSON section and one opaque binary asset. The separate
secure selected-file Import verifier independently authenticates both exact
member bytes and archive identity from that committed file. Replacing it
externally with another valid same-size archive changes the admitted section
but cannot turn same-key Export recovery into a receipt or another write.

A deterministic Linux Export regression now renames and replaces the selected
parent after its directory descriptor has been opened and the anonymous output
fully synced, but before the final file link. The low-level commit reopens the
selected parent and compares device/inode before linking, so preserve-existing
and explicit replacement both refuse with `SelectedParentChanged` without
leaving a named output or damaging the moved original. The process-scoped
Export session retains this as a known no-commit attempt: same-key replay still
cannot write, while a fresh independently admitted key can export to the new
selected directory. A second parent identity observation after directory sync
classifies path drift as `CommittedPathUnverified`, which is treated as an
uncertain prior effect rather than a successful receipt or retry permission.

An additional two-disposition fixture redirects that renamed parent to an
unrelated directory through a symlink; the selected-path no-symlink recheck
rejects it without touching the unrelated target or displaced original.
A zero-byte artifact fixture separately covers the no-write-chunk path: its
second cancellation probe can replace the parent, but the same prelink check
refuses both overwrite modes without creating any file effect.

A subprocess-only relative-path regression changes its working directory
after anonymous staging while keeping the originally opened parent intact.
The selected pathname would now resolve elsewhere; relative Export therefore
rechecks the process CWD device/inode against its initial anchor as well as
checking the parent. Both overwrite modes refuse before linking without
altering the old location or the alternate directory's unrelated file.
A successful unchanged-CWD relative commit is the fixture's positive control.

The process Export retry journal additionally binds the initially observed CWD
device/inode to each relative-target key without moving filesystem ownership
into the pure retry-binding domain. An isolated subprocess first commits a
relative file, then changes CWD to an unrelated directory holding the exact
same ZIP bytes. Same-key recovery now refuses `RelativeCwdChanged` even though
both paths are byte-identical and the newly resolved file matches the archive.

An unchanged-CWD same-key recovery remains authorized by the earlier receipt.
The session also rechecks CWD immediately before and after a new file commit,
conservatively retaining uncertain effect if the postcommit path context drifts.

These checks narrow the observable directory-rename race; they do not lock an
external pathname, prevent changes after either check, or solve the brief
replacement link-before-rename crash window that can leave a named stage.

The profile file writer now captures device/inode identity from its privately
staged Linux file before it is linked into the selected path. A successful
synced commit retains that identity in the process-owned Export attempt.
The read-only target checker includes the re-opened file identity with a
byte-exact match, and same-key recovery requires both original identities.

A red-first session regression for both overwrite dispositions now rejects a
new inode containing identical ZIP bytes as `ExternalTargetDrift`, without
changing either the external replacement or the parked original. An independent
pure classifier test covers changed device and changed inode; a transport test
checks the staged identity equals the final linked target. Stable target
recovery remains successful and never repeats the file effect.

File identity detects observed replacements, not all external mutations. It
does not lock pathnames between observations, rule out inode reuse after the
original file is destroyed, authenticate caller intent, or close the separate
replacement link-before-rename crash window.

A second red-first recovery regression now changes the modification timestamp
of the committed inode without changing its bytes, inode or selected pathname.
Both preserve-existing and explicit-replacement Export retries refuse
`ExternalTargetDrift`; the file remains untouched by the retry. The writer
captures modification and change times from the linked inode only after the
final directory sync, since link/rename operations can change ctime relative to
anonymous staging. The read-only checker returns high-resolution mtime and
ctime from its securely re-opened target; the pure classifier refuses a change
to either timestamp even when digest, length, device and inode still match.

This is an observed-metadata drift guard, not a secure historical file snapshot:
external processes can change the pathname or bytes after the final check,
and file timestamps can be adjusted or reused. Only a trusted caller-owned
Export session can admit requests or return the original synced receipt.

An additional real-process profile CLI regression selects a verified source and
an existing destination that are hardlinks to the same underlying inode.
Default repack refuses `TargetExists` and preserves both names and their bytes;
independently authorized `--replace` instead atomically publishes a distinct
canonical target inode. The verified source retains its original inode and
complete pre-repack bytes; the new output passes independent profile inspection.
This proves explicit overwrite does not silently rewrite the linked source.

The high-resolution change-time check is now covered by a real-file session
fixture that rewrites the *same* complete ZIP bytes through the original inode,
then restores the committed modification timestamp. The test waits for an
independent timestamp tick, proves the path, device, inode, byte content and
mtime match the saved output, then rejects same-key recovery solely because
ctime differs. Both overwrite dispositions leave the externally rewritten file
untouched. Change time narrows, but does not eliminate, mutable-filesystem
history uncertainty.


The Linux profile file-commit transport now captures the selected entry's
regular-or-absent status and, for a regular file, its device, inode, length,
mtime and ctime without following links. It rechecks those observations after
anonymous staging and final cancellation, before any named link. Deterministic
races substitute a symlink or directory for an initially regular target, or
insert a symlink after an absent target; each refuses `InvalidExistingTarget`
and preserves the changed entry and unrelated files.

New red-first fixtures also create an unexpected regular file, swap the selected
inode for another regular file, remove the original, or rewrite that inode to a
different length during staging. These now refuse `SelectedTargetChanged`,
without overwriting the intervening file, recreating the removed name or
leaving a named temporary stage. A real Export session fixture also proves
same-key replay refuses after a changed or newly created target, while a fresh
independent key can authorize a later replacement. Stable replacement remains
successful, and all prelink refusals classify as no file effect.

These checks reduce observed target-substitution risk but do not lock the
entry after the last observation or eliminate the replacement
link-before-rename crash window.

A same-length in-place replacement regression additionally restores the
selected inode's original mtime before the late prelink observation. It
confirms unchanged device, inode, length and mtime with changed ctime, then
requires `SelectedTargetChanged` without publishing Export bytes or losing the
externally rewritten content. This covers the precommit history gate's use of
ctime, not a guarantee against timestamp spoofing or changes after the check.

A red-first Linux writer regression now alters the first 64 KiB of the
anonymous stage through a second process-descriptor handle while the second
64 KiB chunk is still pending. Because the original writer sampled stage
metadata only *after* completing the sync, it previously reported a successful
Export of a complete but byte-different artifact. The transport now uses a
readable anonymous file descriptor and checks the actual staged bytes against
the admitted artifact through bounded 16 KiB reads before linking any name,
then retains its post-read identity, mode and metadata checks. Both overwrite
dispositions reject `StagedFileChanged` without replacing or creating the
selected target.

This does not make a concurrent filesystem mutation or the replacement
link-before-rename crash window impossible; it closes the previously
unobserved pre-sync byte-substitution case.

A second red-first writer fixture extends the anonymous stage beyond the
admitted ZIP length during a two-chunk write. The previous bounded byte
readback matched the entire expected prefix but did not notice the extra
trailing bytes; it linked the oversized file before returning the uncertain
`CommittedPathUnverified` result. The stage's first post-sync metadata check
now requires the exact artifact length as well as private mode and zero links.
Both overwrite dispositions instead refuse as a proven no-commit
`StagedFileChanged`, preserving the original target and avoiding any new name.

This is a prepublication length guard, not an atomic lock on other processes.

A post-sync anonymous-stage truncation regression now forces readback to
encounter an early EOF after the writer observed the original complete length.
The transport classifies this observed mutation as `StagedFileChanged`, not a
generic I/O failure, before publishing either preserve or replacement output.
The original selected target remains intact and no named staging file escapes.

A red-first real CLI fixture also found visually blank Unicode metadata that
the terminal-only escaping rule still emitted literally: Hangul choseong and
jungseong fillers (U+115F/U+1160), braille pattern blank (U+2800), Hangul
filler (U+3164), and halfwidth Hangul filler (U+FFA0). These are now printed as
explicit JSON Unicode escapes in profile identity, optional-feature names,
section paths and JSON text. The independently verified source ZIP remains
byte-exact and unchanged; this is display spoofing hardening, not a new Import
or Export permission, nor a claim to enumerate every invisible Unicode scalar.

A follow-up red-first real CLI case extended the terminal-only display rule
to the remaining default-ignorable Unicode ranges observed in the current
Unicode property inventory, including Khmer and Mongolian formatting,
BMP variation selectors, special-use formatting, shorthand/music formatting,
and the reserved supplementary ignorable range. A complete 4,174-scalar
real CLI fixture pins exact ASCII Unicode/surrogate escape output, no raw
invisible scalars, and unchanged fully SHA-verified source. This table
supplements the Rust control-character predicate, U+2800 braille blank and
line separators;
it is not a guarantee against every possible visually confusable glyph.
